export default "This guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## What Happens to Your Cycle After Birth\n\nDuring pregnancy, the normal menstrual cycle is suppressed by high levels of progesterone and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG). After birth, these hormones drop rapidly. Whether and when menstruation returns depends largely on one factor: prolactin.\n\nProlactin, the hormone that drives milk production, suppresses the hormonal cascade that triggers ovulation. The more frequently and exclusively you breastfeed, the higher prolactin levels tend to be, and the more effectively ovulation is suppressed.\n\nBefore getting into timelines, one important note: **lochia is not a period.** The bleeding that begins immediately after birth and continues for several weeks is the uterus shedding the pregnancy lining. It is a postpartum process, not a menstrual cycle. The return of your actual menstrual period is a separate event.\n\n## Timeline: When to Expect Your Period Back\n\n### If You Are Not Breastfeeding\n\nWithout the prolactin signal from breastfeeding, the reproductive hormone system resumes relatively quickly. Most people who are not breastfeeding see their period return between 6 and 8 weeks postpartum. Some take a bit longer; a return before 6 weeks is uncommon.\n\nOnce the period returns, the cycle may take a few months to stabilize into a consistent pattern. Cycle length, flow, and symptoms can all be different from pre-pregnancy, sometimes permanently and sometimes temporarily.\n\n### If You Are Exclusively Breastfeeding\n\nExclusive, frequent breastfeeding, where the baby is fed only breast milk and nursing sessions occur every few hours including at night, tends to maintain high prolactin levels that effectively suppress ovulation. For people who are exclusively breastfeeding, the period often does not return for 6 months or longer. In some cases it remains absent until weaning or significant reduction in feeding frequency.\n\nThis variability is real: some exclusively breastfeeding people see their period return at 3 months; others do not see it until 18 months. There is no single \"normal.\"\n\n### If You Are Partially Breastfeeding or Supplementing\n\nWhen breastfeeding is supplemented with formula, when pumping replaces some nursing sessions, or when solid foods begin reducing breastfeeding frequency, prolactin levels tend to decrease. The period may return soon after a significant reduction in feeding frequency or after the introduction of supplemental feedings.\n\nNight feeds are often cited as particularly important to prolactin maintenance. When nighttime nursing is dropped or reduced, this sometimes triggers the return of menstruation for people who had not yet seen it.\n\n## What the First Postpartum Period Is Often Like\n\nThe first period after birth can be surprising. Common experiences include:\n\n- **Heavier than usual:** The uterine lining has been building up since pregnancy, and the first shed can be substantial.\n- **More cramping:** Also related to increased prostaglandin production and a lining that may be thicker than your pre-pregnancy norm.\n- **Irregular timing:** The first few postpartum cycles may be shorter or longer than your pre-pregnancy pattern.\n- **Different in character:** Color, texture, and duration may all differ. These differences often normalize within a few cycles.\n\nSome people find their first postpartum period is lighter than before pregnancy; others find it heavier. Both are common.\n\n## Why You Can Get Pregnant Before Your Period Returns\n\nOvulation precedes menstruation. In a typical cycle, ovulation occurs roughly two weeks before the period. This is also true of the first postpartum ovulation: you will ovulate before you see any postpartum period.\n\nIf you have unprotected sex in the postpartum period, even while breastfeeding, even before any period has returned, and ovulation has resumed, pregnancy is possible. Many postpartum pregnancies occur before people realize their fertility has returned.\n\nDiscuss postpartum contraception with your provider, particularly if you want to space pregnancies. Breastfeeding is not a reliable contraceptive method for most people, especially once feeding frequency decreases.\n\n## Tracking in the Postpartum Period\n\nPostpartum cycle tracking looks different from pre-pregnancy tracking. In the early weeks, you are logging lochia, not a period. Once lochia resolves, you may have a gap of weeks or months before any bleeding returns.\n\nWhen bleeding does return, tracking helps you:\n- Distinguish lochia from a true period (lochia should resolve before 6 weeks; later bleeding is more likely a period or spotting related to cycle resumption)\n- Identify when your cycle is establishing itself\n- Spot any patterns that are significantly outside the range described above\n- Build a baseline for your new postpartum cycle pattern\n\nIf you are using any form of fertility awareness method postpartum, tracking alone is not sufficient until you have several months of documented regular ovulatory cycles. The postpartum period requires specific guidance for fertility awareness use; consult a qualified instructor or provider.\n\n## When to Seek Medical Attention\n\n- Heavy postpartum bleeding at any stage (soaking through a pad in under an hour, large clots)\n- Foul-smelling discharge or signs of infection (fever, pain, chills). These may indicate postpartum complications unrelated to cycle return.\n- No period return after 12 months in a person who is not breastfeeding and is not pregnant\n- Significant cycle irregularity that continues beyond the first few cycles after period return\n\n## A Note on Postpartum Data and Privacy\n\nPostpartum health data is sensitive. It reveals recent reproductive history, feeding choices, and recovery details. For people in states where reproductive health data could be subject to legal scrutiny, or for anyone who values keeping health information private, apps that store cycle data on company servers create a record that can be exposed.\n\nPostpartum period tracking data is not covered by HIPAA. A local-first app reduces readable company-side records because core logs stay on your device. Device access, backups, exports, screenshots, and shared copies are still separate risks.";
